2. THE VENDORED `kafka-backup` ENGINE — MIT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
This product shells out to, and redistributes in its RUNNER container image,
the `kafka-backup` binary from osodevops/kafka-backup, which is licensed under
the MIT License. A copy of that licence and the source tarball for the pinned
version are in third_party/.

WHY IT IS OWED: MIT requires the copyright notice and the permission notice to
travel with a redistributed binary. The binary is in the runner image, so the
licence is too — Dockerfile copies third_party/LICENSE-MIT to
/usr/share/licenses/kafka-backup/LICENSE.

WHERE IT IS NOT OWED, AND THEREFORE IS NOT PRESENT: the `weirkeeper`
controller image carries no OSO code of any kind and has no
/usr/share/licenses/kafka-backup/ directory at all. An MIT notice there would
be a claim to redistribute something the image does not contain.
scripts/check-image-weirkeeper.sh check 3 asserts that absence.


3. librdkafka — BSD-2-Clause, STATICALLY LINKED, AND ITS FOURTEEN COMPONENTS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
The `logweir` binary links librdkafka statically, through the `rdkafka-sys`
crate's `cmake-build` feature with `ssl,sasl,zstd,libz`. librdkafka is
Copyright (c) 2012-2022, Magnus Edenhill and 2023, Confluent Inc., under the
BSD-2-Clause licence, and it VENDORS fourteen further components under
licences of their own. BSD-2-Clause requires the copyright notice to travel
with the redistributed binary; the binary is in both container images and in
the release tarballs, so the notice is here.

THIS IS THE ATTRIBUTION `cargo metadata` CANNOT SEE. THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md is
generated from the resolved Rust graph and every crate in it is a Rust crate.
The C code compiled into the same binary appears in that graph as one entry —
`rdkafka-sys` — and the fourteen components below appear in it not at all.

THE COMPONENT LIST BELOW IS DERIVED, NOT MAINTAINED BY HAND. It is read from
`librdkafka/LICENSES.txt` in the resolved `rdkafka-sys` source directory
(under $CARGO_HOME/registry/src/*/rdkafka-sys-<version>/), whose every
`^LICENSE` header names one component.
`crates/logweir/tests/doc_lint.rs::the_notice_names_every_librdkafka_component_in_licenses_txt`
reads that file and asserts this file names each component it finds. An
upstream bump that adds a fifteenth component FAILS that test rather than
passing it, and a component deleted from this list fails it by name. The
spec's own prose and this plan's first draft both said twelve and both omitted
`tinycthread` and `wingetopt`; a hand-maintained list is how that happens.

  cjson            MIT
  crc32c           Zlib
  fnv1a            public domain
  hdrhistogram     MIT
  lz4              BSD-2-Clause
  murmur2          public domain
  nanopb           Zlib
  opentelemetry    Apache-2.0
  pycrc            MIT
  queue            BSD-3-Clause
  regexp           public domain
  snappy           BSD-3-Clause
  tinycthread      Zlib
  wingetopt        ISC

The full text of each is in that same LICENSES.txt, which is the file this
list is derived from; nothing here paraphrases it.


4. OpenSSL — linked through the same feature set
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
librdkafka is built with `ssl` and `sasl`, so the `logweir` binary reaches
OpenSSL for TLS and for SASL mechanisms. The runner image installs `libssl3`
and `libsasl2-2` from Debian; their licence texts are under
/usr/share/doc/libssl3/ and /usr/share/doc/libsasl2-2/ in the image, where
Debian places them. OpenSSL 3.x is Apache-2.0.

The `weirkeeper` controller image has NO OpenSSL: it speaks TLS to exactly one
peer, the Kubernetes API server, through rustls with the `ring` provider, and
installs neither library (Dockerfile.weirkeeper).


